          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            \-Dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifolds"} manifolds
            (possibly and usually equipped with certain structure, notably for instance with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientation"} orientation
            = succeed "-structure" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framing"} framing
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-structure"} G-structure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category"} (∞,n)-category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+cobordisms"} extended cobordisms
            whose
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
                are 0-dimensional (oriented) manifolds (disjoint unions of (oriented) points);
            %li
              %p
                1-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
                are (oriented)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordisms"} cobordisms
                between disjoint unions of (oriented) points;
            %li
              %p 2-morphisms are cobordisms between 1-dimensional cobordisms
            %li
              %p etc.
            %li
              %p
                (n+1)-morphisms are
                %em diffeomorphisms
                between
                = succeed "-dimensional" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %li
              %p (n+2)-morphisms are smooth homotopies of these;
            %li
              %p etc.
          %p
            The
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infinity,n)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+hypothesis"} cobordism hypothesis
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#as_an_fold_complete_segal_space
            As an
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            Here is an outline of the idea of the definition of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Bord
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo ,
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bord_{(\infty,n)}
            as given in (
            %a{:href => "#LurieQFT"}> Lurie
            ) where the main point, apart from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category"} (∞,n)-category
            machinery in the background, is definition 2.2.9.
          %p
            The idea is to start with thinking of
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-fold+category"} n-fold category
            by simply saying that the collection of composites of cobordisms is given by big cobordisms with markings on them, indicating where we think of them as being composed.
          %p
            Let’s first do this for composition in one direction, as in an ordinary 1-category of
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            Consider a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi V
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \hookrightarrow V \times \mathbb{R}
            embedded in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            of the form
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo ×
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \times \mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi V
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi : X \hookrightarrow V \times \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}
            that measures the “height” or maybe better the “length” of the embedded manifold.
          %p
            We can pick a bunch of numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi j
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{t_j \in \mathbb{R}\}
            and think of these as marking a bunch of slices of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^{-1}(t_j)
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %blockquote
            %p (there is an obvious picture to be drawn and uploaded here, maybe somebody finds the time and energy)
          %p
            In this way an embedded manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi V
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \hookrightarrow V \times \mathbb{R}
            and a set of
            = succeed "-numbers" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{t_i\}
            may represent an element in the space of sequences of composable cobordisms. To make this work as expected, the markings on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            may not be too irregular, so we should impose some conditions on what qualifies as a marked manifold. The precise statement is given further below.
          %p
            The collection of these tuples, consisting of an embedded manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi V
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \hookrightarrow V \times \mathbb{R}
            and a collection of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{t_i \in \mathbb{R}\}
            naturally form a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            of the 1-category of
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            To generalize this from just a 1-categorical structure to an
            = succeed "-categorical" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            as before, but now draw markings on it in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            transversal directions, thereby putting a kind of grid on it that subdivides the manifold into cubical slices. A manifold with such subdivision on it may then be regarded as giving an element in the space of
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            To formalize this more general case, we embed
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            not just into a
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo ×
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \times \mathbb{R}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo ×
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \times \mathbb{R}^n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            different coordinate functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi V
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mi i
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_i : X \hookrightarrow V \times \mathbb{R}^n \stackrel{p_i}{\to} \mathbb{R}
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            as having been glued from smaller manifolds.
          %p
            A collection of markings indicating such gluing is now a collection of numbers
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msubsup
                      %mi t
                      %mi j
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msubsup
                      %mi t
                      %mi j
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msubsup
                      %mi t
                      %mi j
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{t_j^1\}, \;\{t_j^2\}, \; \cdots \{t_j^n\}
          %p
            For each direction this yields a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            of such structures, to be thought of as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            of the category of cobordisms under composition in one of these directions. Taken together this is an
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi op
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi op
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Delta^{op} \times \Delta^{op} \times \cdots \times \Delta^{op}
                  \\to Set
          %p
            which is like the nerve of an
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            When suitable regularity conditions are imposed on this data, there is naturally a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            on each of these sets of embedded marked cobordisms, that makes this into an
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi op
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi op
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Top
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Delta^{op} \times \Delta^{op} \times \cdots \times \Delta^{op}
                  \\to Top
                  \\,.
          %p
            To get rid of the dependence of this construction on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            “grow arbitrarily large” by taking the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
            of the above
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            ranges over the finite dimensional subspaces of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^\infty
          %p
            The resulting
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category"} (∞,n)-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Bord
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bord_n
            that we are after. It turns out that it actually is an
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %em complete
            it to an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-fold+complete+Segal+space"} n-fold complete Segal space
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Bord
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi op
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %mi Top
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Bord_n \colon (\Delta^{op})^n \to Top
                  \\,.
          %p
            This, then, is a model for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2Cn%29-category"} (∞,n)-category
            of extended
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %h3#as_a_blob_category
            As a blob
            = succeed "-category" do
